# Target C-II — two-leaf outer energy excludes `t<=1.84362`

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only full-core Type-II strengthening  
**Scope:** full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch  
**Dependencies:** hostile-audited `g=.0077` residual stability; branch-aware strong phase-star capacity; phasewise outer-energy bathtub; common-center two-leaf O--O argument; low-energy cross-phase endpoint theorem  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_two_leaf_outer_energy_exclusion_t184362.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The preceding two-leaf outer-energy theorem excludes

\[
t={q-k\over D}\le1.8436.
\]

Its final energy contradiction has much more room than its endpoint suggests.  The
actual bottleneck is the phase-localization theorem `U,V<0.513`, not the terminal-energy
ledger.  Re-running the same phase-capacity argument on a slightly larger residual box
and keeping deliberately rounded constants gives

\[
\boxed{t\le1.84362\Longrightarrow U,V<0.525-o(1).}
\tag{X1}
\]

At the same endpoint, the retained dominant outer mass still satisfies

\[
z>2.2195-o(1),
\]

which forces every exact phase to retain more than `0.7100D`.  Thus both leaf phases
carry more than `0.185D` retained O--O pair weight.  After the common-center bad-weight
loss, each leaf still has more than `0.149D` high-center-degree O--O weight.  The same
triangle-free/nonalternating-endpoint mechanism then forces enough leaf terminal
energy that, after adding only the disjoint center one-well energy, the complete outer
detour budget is exceeded by more than `0.004D`.

Therefore no full-core Type-II survivor can satisfy

\[
\boxed{t\le1.84362.}
\tag{X2}
\]

Equivalently, at the current proof-only full-core scope,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{q-k\over D}\ge1.84362.
}
\tag{X3}
\]

No Level-I closure is claimed.

---

## 1. Residual slack at `t=1.84362`

Use the same audited residual variables as the `1.8436` theorem:

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
C_{77}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0077,
\]

\[
h=U_{net}-C_{77},
\qquad
x={\kappa\over2},
\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D},
\]

and

\[
2\delta+x+h
=R_t:={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_{77}.
\tag{1.1}
\]

Shared retention and residual incidence use the same constant

\[
c=1.05.
\]

Set

\[
T={92181\over50000}=1.84362,
\qquad
H={7\over25000}=0.00028.
\]

At `t=T`, `h=H`, `x=0`, put

\[
\delta_H={R_T-H\over2},
\]

\[
B_H={c^2H^2\over2(1-\delta_H)},
\qquad
A_H=\pi-{T\over2}-cH-B_H.
\]

The exact certificate proves

\[
F(A_H)-\delta_H>2.32\times10^{-5},
\qquad
F(s)=s-6\sin(s/6).
\tag{1.2}
\]

The parent residual-capacity derivatives remain strictly negative on the enlarged box:

\[
Q_h<-2.5,
\qquad
Q_x<-2.7.
\tag{1.3}
\]

Hence any putative survivor with `t<=T` satisfies

\[
\boxed{0\le h<H+o(1).}
\tag{1.4}
\]

This is the only enlargement of the residual window needed below.

---

## 2. Dominant phase box

From (1.4), the dominant exact three-phase orbit mass

\[
W=C+U+V
\]

satisfies

\[
\boxed{W<1.912606+o(1).}
\tag{2.1}
\]

The total exact-orbit residual outside the dominant orbit is at most `cH+o(1)`, so the
dominant strict-rhombus crossing mass satisfies

\[
\boxed{E_{dom}>0.9068995-o(1).}
\tag{2.2}
\]

Both numbers are outward rounded in the exact certificate.  In particular

\[
1.912606-\left(C_{77}+0.00028\right)>3.8\times10^{-7},
\]

and the crossing lower bound has positive exact slack.

---

## 3. Both leaves have train mass below `0.525D`

Write the dominant phase masses in decreasing order

\[
C\ge U\ge V.
\]

Suppose for contradiction that

\[
U\ge U_0:=0.525.
\tag{3.1}
\]

Use the adverse rounded total mass

\[
W_0=1.912606
\]

and crossing target

\[
E_0=0.9068995.
\]

Generic center capacity already excludes

\[
C\le U_0
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
C\ge1.044.
\tag{3.2}
\]

There are two remaining center ranges.

### 3.1 Low center

Put

\[
C_0=W_0-2U_0=0.862606.
\]

For `C<=C_0`, the larger-leaf condition does not improve the balanced-leaf endpoint,
so the hostile-audited strong aggregate phase-star capacity applies.  With

\[
K={2\over\sqrt3},
\]

write

\[
\Phi(C)
=C(W_0-C)
-{1\over10}\left(3-{W_0\over C}\right)
\left({W_0+C\over2}-K\right)^2.
\tag{3.3}
\]

The same derivative calculation as in the parent two-leaf theorem is increasing up to
`C_0` on this branch.  The exact certificate obtains

\[
\Phi(C_0)<E_0-0.0054.
\tag{3.4}
\]

Thus the low-center branch is impossible.

### 3.2 Main center interval

Now

\[
0.862606<C<1.044.
\]

The strong physical pair theorem is valid for both center/leaf pairs throughout this
box.  The worst strong-branch ratio is below `0.792`, while

\[
\sqrt{20/29}>0.8304.
\tag{3.5}
\]

The hostile-audited correlated phase capacity is increasing with total orbit mass and
decreasing with the larger leaf mass.  Hence the adverse choice is

\[
W=W_0,
\qquad U=U_0,
\qquad V=W_0-C-U_0.
\]

Define

\[
\begin{aligned}
G(C)=&\ C(U_0+V)\\
&-{1\over10}\left(1-{V\over C}\right)(C+U_0-K)^2\\
&-{1\over10}\left(1-{U_0\over C}\right)(C+V-K)^2.
\end{aligned}
\tag{3.6}
\]

The exact derivative identity gives

\[
G''(C)<-2
\tag{3.7}
\]

throughout the interval.  At

\[
c_*=0.931922
\]

the standard tangent bound for a function with second derivative at most `-2` gives

\[
\max_CG(C)
\le G(c_*)+{G'(c_*)^2\over4}.
\]

Outward rational evaluation proves

\[
\boxed{
\max_CG(C)<E_0-1.47\times10^{-5}.
}
\tag{3.8}
\]

This contradicts (2.2).  Therefore

\[
\boxed{U<0.525-o(1),\qquad V<0.525-o(1).}
\tag{3.9}
\]

