## 4. Retained dominant outer mass exceeds `2.2195D`

The retained dominant outer-occurrence mass `z`, complement retained O--O pair mass
`p`, and complement terminal energy `e_c` obey the same audited system used by the
`1.8436` theorem:

\[
z+p\ge\pi-{t\over2}-x-ch-o(1),
\tag{4.1}
\]

\[
p(1-\delta)-{e_c\over2}
\le{c^2h^2\over2}+o(1),
\tag{4.2}
\]

\[
F(z)+e_c\le\delta+o(1).
\tag{4.3}
\]

Assume

\[
z\le Z:=2.2195.
\]

As in the parent theorem, capped retention is increasing in `z`.  On the
`delta>=F(Z)` side it is driven to the boundary `delta=F(Z)` and then to `x=0`.  The
corresponding residual value is

\[
h_*=R_T-2F(Z).
\]

The exact enclosure gives

\[
0<h_*<0.000232<0.00028.
\tag{4.4}
\]

At that point the capped retention excess has outward upper bound

\[
\boxed{
Z+{c^2h_*^2\over2(1-F(Z))}
-\pi+{T\over2}+ch_*
<-3.91\times10^{-5}.
}
\tag{4.5}
\]

The `delta<F(Z)` branch has the same negative `Q_h,Q_x` derivatives from (1.3), so it
cannot do better than the boundary already excluded.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{z>2.2195-o(1).}
\tag{4.6}

---

## 5. Every exact phase retains more than `0.7100D`

Write

\[
z=z_C+z_U+z_V
\]

and

\[
f(s)=s-2\sin(s/2).
\]

Phasewise one-well energy gives

\[
\delta\ge f(z_C)+f(z_U)+f(z_V)-o(1).
\tag{5.1}
\]

From (1.1) and `x,h>=0`,

\[
\delta\le{R_T\over2}+o(1).
\tag{5.2}
\]

If one retained phase mass were at most

\[
a=0.7100,
\]

strict convexity makes the cheapest remaining split equal, giving

\[
f(z_C)+f(z_U)+f(z_V)
\ge
f(0.7100)
+2f\left({2.2195-0.7100\over2}\right)-o(1).
\]

The certificate proves that this exceeds `R_T/2` by more than

\[
3.9\times10^{-6}.
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{z_C,z_U,z_V>0.7100-o(1).}
\tag{5.3}

---

## 6. Both leaves contain large retained O--O populations

For each leaf phase `i`, retained terminal weights satisfy

\[
z_i\le M_i+P_i+o(1),
\]

where `M_i` is its train mass and `P_i` is retained O--O pair weight.  Combining
(3.9) and (5.3),

\[
\boxed{
P_U,P_V>0.7100-0.525-o(1)
=0.185-o(1).
}
\tag{6.1}

Let a leaf O--O train be bad when it crosses at most half of the center-phase trains.
The same center/leaf missing-pair correlation used in the parent theorem bounds the
**total** bad retained weight across both leaves by

\[
{2[C(W-C)-E_{dom}]\over C-U}.
\]

Using the adverse constants `W_0,E_0,U_0`, the assertion that this is below

\[
B=0.036
\]

is equivalent to positivity of

\[
B(C-U_0)-2[C(W_0-C)-E_0].
\]

The exact discriminant is negative:

\[
\operatorname{disc}<-0.00106.
\tag{6.2}
\]

Therefore the total bad pair weight is below `0.036D`, and even if all of it is spent
against one leaf, each leaf retains good pair weight

\[
\boxed{G_U,G_V>0.149-o(1).}
\tag{6.3}

---

## 7. Cross-leaf noncrossing forces too much outer energy

Take one good `U`-leaf O--O train and one good `V`-leaf O--O train.  Both cross more
than half of the same center family, so they have a common center neighbor.  If the two
leaf trains crossed, these three trains would form a triangle in the stopped-train
crossing graph.  That graph is triangle-free.  Hence every good cross-leaf pair is
noncrossing.

The two leaf phases differ by exactly `pi/3`.  The audited cross-phase endpoint theorem
therefore gives

\[
e_\tau+e_\sigma\ge2-\sqrt3-o(1)
\]

for every good pair.  Summing the complete weighted product as in the parent theorem
gives

\[
\boxed{
E_U+E_V
\ge(2-\sqrt3)(0.149)-o(1).
}
\tag{7.1}

These terminal payments lie on the two leaf selected edge populations.  The center
phase selected edge population is disjoint, and (5.3) gives it one-well energy at
least

\[
f(0.7100)-o(1).
\tag{7.2}

Consequently the total selected dominant outer energy is at least

\[
(2-\sqrt3)(0.149)+f(0.7100)-o(1).
\tag{7.3}

The exact certificate uses `sqrt(3)<1.7321` and proves

\[
\boxed{
(2-\sqrt3)(0.149)+f(0.7100)
>{R_T\over2}+0.00434.
}
\tag{7.4}

But all selected dominant outer energy is paid from `delta`, while (5.2) gives
`delta<=R_T/2+o(1)`.  This is impossible.

Thus no full-core Type-II survivor satisfies `t<=1.84362`.

---

## 8. Exact certificate and frontier

Run

```sh
python3 research/verify_target_c_two_leaf_outer_energy_exclusion_t184362.py
```

The exact certificate uses `Fraction` arithmetic, Machin enclosures for `pi`, integer
square-root enclosures, and alternating Taylor bounds for `sin`.  Its load-bearing
margins include

```text
residual localization                  > 2.32e-5
low-center phase capacity              > 5.40e-3
main-interval phase capacity           > 1.47e-5
capped-retention contradiction         > 3.91e-5
phase-imbalance contradiction          > 3.93e-6
final outer-energy contradiction       > 4.34e-3
```

The final margin remains macroscopic.  Exploratory optimization indicates that this
particular rounded argument can be pushed somewhat beyond `1.84362`, but its limiting
currency is the rapid deterioration of the provable larger-leaf train-mass ceiling,
not the final endpoint-energy inequality.  No stronger decimal is claimed here.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only full-core Type-II strengthening to `t>=1.84362`.
C-II and Level I remain open.
